ifferent months.\n\nFor 1986, the pattern continues with expenditures ranging from 70 to 113. The lowest points appear in January and May-July, while the higher values cluster around early and late year months. This consistent variability underscores the complex financial landscape of the judiciary during these years.\n\nI'll proceed with computing the geometric mean across these values, carefully tracking the data points to ensure precise calculation.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_8259d7926b6f014f","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\nimport math\n\n# Monthly judiciary outlays from Jan 1984 to Mar 1987\n# All values in millions of dollars\n\n# 1984\noutlays_1984 = [84, 89, 63, 74, 62, 93, 65, 79, 69, 79, 67, 96]\n\n# 1985\noutlays_1985 = [66, 60, 59, 119, 75, 63, 74, 103, 66, 98, 74, 102]\n\n# 1986 (using consistent values from the 1987 bulletin for Dec 1986: 118)\noutlays_1986 = [76, 109, 74, 129, 75, 71, 70, 106, 87, 73, 73, 118]\n\n# 1987 (Jan-Mar)\noutlays_1987 = [79, 76, 110]\n\n# Combine all outlays\nall_outlays = outlays_1984 + outlays_1985 + outlays_1986 + outlays_1987\n\nprint(f\"Number of months: {len(all_outlays)}\")\nprint(f\"Outlays: {all_outlays}\")\n\n# Calculate geometric mean\n# GM = (product of all values)^(1/n)\n# Using natural log: GM = exp(sum(ln(x))/n)\n\nproduct = 1\nfor val in all_outlays:\n    product *= val\n    \nprint(f\"Product: {product}\")\n\n# Using Python's math.pow\nn = len(all_outlays)\ngeometric_mean = math.pow(product, 1/n)\n\nprint(f\"Geometric mean: {geometric_mean}\")\n\n# Alternatively using logs\nlog_sum = sum(math.log(x) for x in all_outlays)\ngm_log = math.exp(log_sum / n)\nprint(f\"Geometric mean (log method): {gm_log}\")\n\n# Round to nearest thousandth in millions\n# The values are already in millions, so just round\nprint(f\"\\nRounded to nearest thousandth: {round(gm_log, 3)}\")\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metada